  "body": "### What problem are you trying to solve?\n\nIt is a hassle to override CSS, and I have to write `!important` on each line of CSS. But I want a way to tell the browser to override all the CSS with this new file.\n\nIf I have a theme on my website, but I want to change the theme without replace the CSS tag or modifying it, especially if use a CMS. So anything in my CSS file would overwrite everything else\n\n### What solutions exist today?\n\nThe only solution I’ve found so far is to add `!important` to every CSS declaration, which quickly becomes unmanageable when revamping or restyling a website.\n\nAnother workaround is to remove the existing CSS file entirely using JavaScript:\n\n```js\ndocument.querySelector('link[href=\"theme.css\"]')?.remove();\n ```\n\nHowever, this approach eliminates *all* styles from the old theme — including properties I still want to keep, such as button shapes, spacing, or base layout.\n\nWhat I really need is a way to add a new stylesheet that simply **overrides** existing styles without having to rewrite every rule with `!important` or completely discard the original CSS.\n\n### How would you solve it?\n\n\n\nMy solution is to add a new attribute to the `<link>` element — such as `important`  — that defines the stylesheet’s override priority. For example, you could write:\n\n ```html\n <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"theme.css\" important=\"1\">\n <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"custom.css\" important=\"2\">\n```\n\nThe higher the number (or the presence of `important`), the stronger the stylesheet’s priority in the cascade.\n\n\n\n### Anything else?\n\nThis is an example\nhttps://github.com/Bashamega/proposal-html-important",
